  "name": "Question 6",
  "problem": "$\\,$ $x\\in R$\n\nThe predicate $P(x)$ is given above. Which of following represents $\u00acP(x)?$",
  "worked_solution": "In plain English, our predicate $P(x)$ means the following: \n\n$\\,$ $x$ is a member of the set of real numbers.\n\nThe symbol \"$\u00ac$\" represents negation. To negate our plain English expression, we place the words \"It is not true that\" in front of it. This gives the following:\n\n$\\,$ It is not true that $x$ is a member of the set of real numbers.\n\nIn other words, $x$ is *not* a member of $R.$ Therefore, $\u00acP(x)$ means the following:\n\n$\\,$ $x\\notin R$",
